1. Scope and prior rights

"Licensor" means each copyright holder who has authorized these terms for
their material, only to the extent of that holder's rights. "Covered Material"
means Gitrochi code and documentation expressly identified in the adoption
record or a subsequent authorized contribution as licensed under these terms.
"You" means the individual or legal entity exercising these permissions.

This license applies only after adoption and only to Covered Material.
Inclusion in this repository does not by itself establish the authority to
relicense a contribution. Material without the necessary authorization retains
its applicable prior license and is not made exclusively subject to this one.

Gitrochi was previously released under the MIT License. The original notice and
permission text is preserved in LICENSE-MIT. Existing MIT permissions, including
permission to sell, modify, distribute and sublicense MIT-covered material,
remain available under their terms, including for lawful downstream recipients.
This license does not retroactively restrict prior versions or material also
available under MIT. The review baseline is recorded in docs/licensing-review.md;
it is not a declaration that earlier contributors have agreed to relicense.

Third-party code, libraries, fonts, icons and other separately licensed material
are excluded. Their own licenses control their use, modification, redistribution
and source availability. Nothing here limits rights separately granted by those
licenses, applicable law, or GitHub's Terms of Service, including rights to view
and fork a public repository using GitHub's functionality.

2. Free personal and organizational use

Subject to these terms, Licensor grants You a worldwide, non-exclusive,
royalty-free permission to download, install, run, study, copy, build and privately
modify Covered Material for Your personal use or Your organization's internal
business operations, including commercial work. There is no license fee for
individuals or businesses.

An organization may provide internal copies to its employees and contractors
solely to perform work on its behalf, under these same restrictions. Contractors
receive no right to redistribute Gitrochi as their own product. You need not
publish private changes or submit a PR merely to use Gitrochi.

Your own repositories, commits, documents and other ordinary work output do not
become Covered Material merely because You create or manage them using Gitrochi.
You may use and deliver that work commercially under terms You choose, provided
it does not incorporate Covered Material requiring separate permission.

3. Sharing official releases and contributing changes

You may share an unchanged official Gitrochi release without charging for it,
provided You include its license and attribution notices, preserve all required
third-party notices, identify the Official Repository as its source, and do not
represent Yourself as the official publisher. Linking to official downloads is
encouraged. A renamed, repackaged or modified product is not an unchanged release.

You may create development branches and contribution forks, publish changes in
them for review, and submit patches or code contributions by pull request to the
Official Repository under CONTRIBUTING.md and the applicable contribution
agreement. This permission includes collaboration and automated checks necessary
for that official contribution process. A contribution fork does not authorize
an independently released product, package feed, installer or derivative project.

Rights separately granted by GitHub's Terms of Service remain unaffected whether
or not a particular fork leads to a PR. Nothing here obliges You to contribute,
or obliges maintainers to accept or merge a contribution.

4. Restrictions on Covered Material

Except as expressly permitted above or by separate written authorization from
the relevant rights holders, You may not:

  a. sell, resell, rent, lease, or charge a fee for copies of Gitrochi or access
     to Gitrochi itself, including as a paid bundle or a rebranded product;
  b. distribute, publish or release Covered Material or modified versions as an
     independent project, forked product, library, package, installer or service
     containing Covered Material, whether free or paid, outside the official
     contribution process;
  c. sublicense Covered Material, or grant others rights beyond this license;
  d. remove or misrepresent copyright, authorship, license or origin notices.

These restrictions concern Gitrochi itself; they do not prohibit being paid for
ordinary work performed using Gitrochi as permitted by section 2. They also do
not restrict independently authored work containing no Covered Material, prior
MIT rights, third-party rights, or permissions preserved in section 1.

5. Attribution and names

Keep this license, the existing copyright notices, LICENSE-MIT where applicable,
and required third-party notices in permitted copies. Preserve existing credits
and references to the Official Repository. Identify modifications in contribution
branches so they are not mistaken for official releases.

This license grants no trademark right or permission to imply endorsement. The
Gitrochi name may be used truthfully to identify the project or a contribution.

6. Contributions and exceptions

Code and documentation contributions are accepted only through pull requests to
the Official Repository, with the express, versioned assent process in
CONTRIBUTING.md and CLA.md. Submission alone is not treated as a copyright
assignment or as assent to a separate CLA. Existing contributors are not deemed
to have agreed retroactively.

Request exceptions through the licensing contacts above. Any exception must be
written and issued by holders authorized over the affected material; repository
maintenance access alone does not prove that authority. Silence is not consent.

7. Termination

Permissions for Covered Material terminate if You breach these conditions. They
are reinstated if You remedy the breach within 30 days after first receiving
notice of it, unless You previously used this cure for the same type of breach.
This does not terminate separate MIT or third-party permissions. Provisions on
prior rights, attribution, disclaimers and accrued obligations survive.

